WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.800
Netflix has a bad habit to kick.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.260
And that habit can be summed up in one word:

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.440
"SMOKIN!"

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.040
After a recent report revealed that depictions
of smoking in its original content have tripled

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:18.830
in just the last year, the streamer has declared
that they will adopt a new, much more restrictive

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:21.640
policy in regard to such depictions going
forward.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.500
The move comes just days after the release
of the third season of the hit series Stranger

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.289
Things, which happens to feature a ton of
era-appropriate smoking.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:32.930
"Yeah, company policy is a real drag."

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:37.240
The report was released by Truth Initiative,
a non-profit organization that tracks the

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.000
appearances of tobacco products in major media.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.960
It's not the first time that the organization
has had occasion to call out Netflix, or even

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.480
Stranger Things, for that matter.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.420
A similar report released in March 2018 took
the streamer to task for featuring 182 "smoking

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.320
incidents" across the ten episodes which comprised
the show's first season, with other original

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.860
offerings such as House of Cards and Orange
is the New Black also coming under fire.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.280
The earlier report also pointed out the troubling
fact that streaming services which aren't

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.030
bound to the same content standards that govern
traditional broadcast television are extremely

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.640
popular among young people ages 15 to 24,
an age group coveted as potential customers

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.299
by tobacco companies.

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.240
The new analysis pointed out that the trend
of featuring characters who light up on a

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.720
regular basis hasn't just continued, it's
exploded:

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.899
"Based on estimated viewership of these programs,
results suggest that approximately 28 million

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:35.400
young people were exposed to tobacco through
television and streaming programs in these

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.330
most popular shows alone.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.340
That exposure is a significant public health
concern."

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.790
When the issue first reared its head last
year, Netflix issued what could be described

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.790
as a non-response:

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:50.950
"While streaming entertainment is more popular
than ever, we're glad that smoking is not.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.610
We're interested to find out more about the
study."

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.740
This new missive, however, appears to have
gotten the streaming giant's attention.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.810
As viewers were watching Stranger Things'
police chief Jim Hopper puffing away throughout

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:05.780
the latest batch of episodes, Netflix was
putting together a detailed statement, which

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.400
it released to Entertainment Weekly.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.790
It read, in part,

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.450
"Netflix strongly supports artistic expression.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.220
We also recognize that smoking is harmful
and when portrayed positively on screen can

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.550
adversely influence young people.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:23.860
Going forward, all new projects that we commission
with ratings of TV-14 or below for series

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.630
or PG-13 or below for films, will be smoking
and e-cigarette free except for reasons of

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.070
historical or factual accuracy.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:36.610
For new projects with higher ratings, there'll
be no smoking or e-cigarettes unless it's

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:42.070
essential to the creative vision of the artist
or because it's character-defining (historically

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.680
or culturally important)."

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.090
The statement also revealed that, in addition
to making a concerted effort to cut down on

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.830
the butts, there would be changes forthcoming
to its content warnings.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:56.370
"Starting later this year, smoking information
will be included as part of our ratings on

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.530
the Netflix service so our members can make
informed choices about what they watch."

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.090
After nearly two decades of steadily falling
numbers, use of tobacco products among teens

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.510
has recently spiked.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.650
To be clear, nobody is pinning the spike on
Netflix.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:14.920
The reason for the surge is the soaring popularity
of e-cigarettes such as the Juul, which have

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:20.290
seen an explosion in use by teens and young
adults while the numbers for combustible cigarettes

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.310
have remained about the same.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.650
It's worth noting that the decades-long drop
in the number of teen smokers came during

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.510
a time when references to tobacco products
were being carefully stripped away from broadcast

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:32.510
television.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:38.290
So it's certainly possible that the more smoking-friendly
streaming services which now field more original

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:43.330
series than broadcast networks for the first
time ever could contribute, in some small

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:47.260
way at least, to a plateauing or even a reversal
of the trend.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.750
Stranger things have happened.

